00:00:03.110 --> 00:00:04.550 align:start position:0%
coral reefs occupy less than a quarter  of 1% of the ocean  get our home to a quarter of all marine  life each coral is comprised of hundreds  to thousands of individual polyps all  attached to a rock-hard skeleton that  they form but exactly how corals  construct these stony skeletons has long  been debated is it a straightforward  chemical process of reflects  environmental conditions or the coral  polyps exert some control through their  biology to answer this question  researchers studied stylophora pissed  Alana a widespread stony coral found in  the Indian and Pacific Oceans using  high-tech imaging they found that corals  secrete acid rich proteins which  encourage the precipitation of a form of  calcium carbonate known as Eragon I each  day the corals secrete enough of these  proteins to leave more than 5 microns  with stoney coating if you watch the  coral growing in a tank at this rate  you'd see at least one millimeter of  growth in 250 days researchers also  found that these acid rich proteins can  function within a relatively wide pH  range and may be able to adapt to new  conditions as the ocean home becomes  more acidic due to climate change  because of their control over the  protein secretion and the ability to  respond or not different conditions  these findings strongly suggest that  coral skeleton building is largely  biological coral reefs can still be  affected by other factors such as rising  ocean temperatures and nutrient  pollution but at least for now we know  corals are the captain of their own ship  [Music]  Oh  [Music]
 

